<title>USA: Aviall Inc Q1 net shr profit vs loss.</title>
<headline>Aviall Inc Q1 net shr profit vs loss.</headline>
<dateline>DALLAS 1997-04-22</dateline>
<text>
<p>Periods Ended March 31, 1997 and 1996</p>
<p>		 (Dollars in thousands, except per share data)</p>
<p>							    First Quarter</p>
<p>							1997	 1996 (c)</p>
<p> Net sales					$91,682	  92,168</p>
<p> Cost of sales				   68,638	  67,572</p>
<p> Gross profit				    23,044	  24,596</p>
<p>Operating and other expenses:</p>
<p> Selling and administrative expenses     16,191	  21,474</p>
<p> Nonrecurring items (a)		     (1,436)	   3,850</p>
<p> Interest expense				   734	   2,763</p>
<p> Earnings (loss) before income taxes	7,555	 (3,491)</p>
<p> Provision for income taxes		     612	     236</p>
<p> Net earnings (loss)			    6,943	 (3,727)</p>
<p> Net earnings (loss) per share (b)	   0.35	  (0.19)</p>
<p>Weighted average common and</p>
<p> common equivalent shares		19,694,428    19,457,739</p>
<p>(a)  The $1.4 million nonrecurring credit resulted from the repayment in January 1997 of an unsecured subordinated note. The company earned $0.28 per share before the gain. The $3.9 million nonrecurring charge in 1996 was due to Aviall's decision to sell its aerospace fastener business.</p>
<p>(b)  If Aviall had computed net earnings (loss) per share in accordance with the provisions of Statement of Financial Accounting Standards No. 128, &quot;Earnings per Share,&quot; the pro forma basic and diluted net earnings (loss) per share for 1997 and 1996 would have been $0.35 and ($0.19), respectively.</p>
<p>(c)  The 1996 results include the aerospace fastener business which was sold in the third quarter of 1996.</p>
<p>((-- New York Newsdesk 212 859-1610))</p>
</text>
